# Build-context exclusions for the Cloud Run image (see Dockerfile).
#
# The Dockerfile copies whole directories (`packages/shared/`, `apps/core-api/`),
# so anything not excluded here is uploaded to the builder and can also
# invalidate a layer. node_modules alone is ~1GB across this workspace.
#
# Excluding a build INPUT here breaks the image, so the rule is: exclude
# generated output and local state, never source or manifests.

# Installed trees — every stage runs its own `npm ci`. A host node_modules is
# also the wrong platform (darwin binaries for ffmpeg-static and esbuild).
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Build output — the build stage regenerates dist/ from source. A stale host
# dist/ copied in would be silently overwritten, but it still busts the cache.
dist/
**/dist/
apps/docs/.astro/

# VCS and CI. COMMIT_SHA arrives as a build arg, so .git is not needed.
.git/
.gitignore
.github/

# Local tooling and editor state
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store

# Env files. None are read at build time, and baking one into an image layer
# is how secrets leak into a registry.
.env
.env.*

# Deploy and tooling configs that mean nothing to this image. The Cloud Run
# runtime env lives in deploy/, which is not copied in at all.
firebase.json
.firebaserc
firestore.indexes.json
firestore.rules
storage.rules
wrangler.jsonc

# Docs, specs, and the reference app — none are inputs to the core-api bundle.
# Their package.json files are the exception and must survive: `npm ci` compares
# the lockfile against EVERY declared workspace and fails as out-of-sync if one
# is missing, even though this image never builds them. Docker applies these
# rules in order, so the negations below re-include exactly those two files.
specs/
lexicons/
eslint-rules/
apps/docs/
!apps/docs/package.json
apps/reference/
!apps/reference/package.json
*.md

# Tests are bundled out by esbuild (only src/index.ts is an entry point) but
# they are still context upload and cache churn.
**/*.test.ts
